Counterexamples to V: If devices have different physical sizes, pinning a group at creation could assign a large object to four small devices. These fill quickly while larger devices remain underused. Writes to the object fail despite available space elsewhere. Another case: a device in the pinned group fails after creation. The system must rebuild parity from remaining devices, but if the failed device was critical for parity reconstruction due to its position, recovery might fail unexpectedly. Pinning also prevents dynamic adjustment when new devices are added, forcing data to stay on older devices even when new ones offer better reliability.

Group choice becomes stale when devices fail or fill unevenly. If a device in the pinned group fails, subsequent writes to that object may fail if parity rebuilds are blocked. If devices fill unevenly (e.g., small devices fill faster), writes to the pinned group fail despite free space on other devices. If a device's reliability degrades (e.g., bad sectors), the system cannot move data out of the pinned group, increasing failure risk.

When a new device is added, background rebalance moves data. For a pinned group, data movement depends on implementation. If rebalance avoids moving pinned object data, the new device receives data only from non-pinned objects. This leaves the pinned group's devices overused while the new device remains underutilized. The measured 78.6% no-data-loss figure no longer holds because the imbalance increases failure risk for pinned objects.

Continuous object creation/deletion can cause imbalance. If many large objects are pinned to the same four devices during creation, those devices fill completely. New objects are allocated to remaining devices, but the full devices cannot accept more data. The allocator must either reject writes or forcibly move pinned data (violating V), causing inconsistency.

Reject V. The narrowed version: choose the four emptiest devices for every stripe, not just at creation. This matches the measured data where per-stripe selection achieves 77.2% no-data-loss under skewed workloads with zero spread. It avoids capacity issues from device size disparitiesifferences, dynamically balances usage, and ensures all devices are utilized efficiently during rebalance.
